---

## testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Audit

**Target:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10  
**Integration calibration applied:** Class is annotated `[TestCategory("Integration")]` — Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and Sleepy Test severities are downgraded per calibration rules.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 2 | 2 |
| Medium | 2 | 2 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

*3 of 7 test methods are smell-free.*

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

**Academic name:** Conditional Test Logic  
**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);   // path A
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);      // path B
}
```

**Problem:** The `if`/`else` means one assertion path is *never exercised* in a given run. If the production code silently changes the return type, the untested branch rots without warning.

**Fix:** Split into two deterministic tests:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden coverage gap — one branch is a dead path, giving false confidence.

---

#### ❷ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

**Academic name:** Unknown Test / Empty Test  
**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Note:** The method name implies a "does not throw" intent, so this *may* be deliberate. However, MSTest already fails on any unhandled exception — this test adds zero verification beyond that. If `Insert` silently discards rows, this test still passes.

**Fix:** Assert on the observable outcome:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — test passes even if `Insert` silently drops records or corrupts data.

---

#### ❸ Sleepy Test — **Medium** *(downgraded from High; integration context)*

**Academic name:** Sleepy Test  
**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    Thread.Sleep(3000);   // ← blocks thread for 3 seconds

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Additional note:** This is an `async Task` method using synchronous `Thread.Sleep` instead of `await Task.Delay`, which blocks the thread-pool thread unnecessarily.

**Calibration:** Downgraded from High → Medium because the class is a declared integration test. Sleeps are more tolerable in integration tests that coordinate with real asynchronous processes. Still a smell because fixed waits are non-deterministic — on a slow CI agent, 3 s may not be enough; on a fast machine, it wastes 3 s unnecessarily.

**Fix:** Poll with a timeout instead of sleeping:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures in CI, and a 3-second unconditional wait slows every test run.

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

**Academic name:** Assertion Roulette  
**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);            // no message
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);   // no message
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email); // no message
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);        // no message
```

**Problem:** Four assertions, zero descriptive messages. When one fails in CI, the log says `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>.` — you must open the source to understand *which property* was wrong and *why it matters*.

**Fix:** Add assertion messages:

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip through insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip through insert.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Inserted user should receive a positive database ID.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis, especially in CI logs without source context.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

Three methods are clean and well-structured:

| Method | Why it's good |
|--------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused on one behavior, 2 assertions with clear intent |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion, verifies the *absence* of the record — crisp negative test |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Good concurrency stress test, single count assertion, `await` used correctly |

`ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` is also clean — the `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` is self-documenting (3 items were just inserted), so it is **not** flagged as Magic Number Test.

---

### Smells Scanned but Not Found (15 / 19)

| Smell | Status |
|-------|--------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean — in-memory SQLite is hermetic, no external resources |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean — no method calls 4+ distinct production methods; integration context applies |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ Clean — all literals are contextually self-documenting |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean — no `ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Clean — no `try`/`catch` blocks |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by all 7 tests |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` attributes |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Clean — each test targets a distinct behavior |
| Constructor Initialization | ✅ Clean — uses `[TestInitialize]` properly |
| Default Test | ✅ Clean — class is meaningfully named |
| Redundant Print | ✅ Clean — no `Console.Write` calls |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ Clean — no tautological assertions |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ Clean — in-memory database, no assumed external files |
| Empty Test | ✅ Clean — no completely empty test bodies |

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Impact |
